  C("Which pair of sentences expresses a single proposition, according to Feser?", ["“Snow is white” and “Snow is cold”", "“Mars has two moons” and “Mars has three moons”", "“2 + 2 = 4” and “2 + 2 = 5”", "“John is a bachelor” and “John is an unmarried man”"], 3, "Different sentences can express one proposition; Feser’s examples include the bachelor pair and “Snow is white” / “Schnee ist weiss.”", "recall"),
  C("Which statement about possible worlds matches Feser’s account?", ["They are physically existing parallel universes", "They are limited by the actual laws of physics", "They are ways things could have been, limited by logic rather than physics", "They are simply sentences in books about alternate history"], 2, "Physicists consider different laws, so possibility here is not physical possibility; but logic rules out 2 + 2 = 5 and round squares.", "understand"),
  C("When Socrates and Obama both think that snow is white, what is shared?", ["One proposition, though the acts of thinking and the languages differ", "Only a sentence", "The same brain state", "Nothing — each has a private thought"], 0, "Two acts of thinking, one content. That shared content is the proposition, and its shareability later refutes conceptualism.", "understand")
